#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --quiet --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.12"
# dependencies = ["agent-client-protocol==0.12.1", "httpx>=0.28,<1"]
# ///
"""omnigent-acp — an ACP agent (stdio) whose sessions are Omnigent sessions.

    omnigent-acp [--agent NAME_OR_ID] [--host auto|NAME|ID] [--url URL]
                 [--keep-t3-instructions] [--t3-tools] [--trace FILE]

Lets an ACP client (T3 Code's "Local command" ACP provider) drive Omnigent:
Omnigent stays the owner of every session — harness, terminal, policies,
children — and this process only translates one client's view of it.

Semantics:
  * One ACP session maps to one Omnigent session, created lazily on the first
    prompt so that clients that open throwaway sessions (T3 Code's model probe)
    leave nothing behind. The ACP session id is recorded on the Omnigent session
    as the ``t3.acp_session_id`` label, which is how ``session/load`` finds it
    (among the agent's 2000 most recently updated sessions; Omnigent has no
    label filter). A load that finds none fails rather than starting afresh, and
    a found one replays its messages and tool calls. T3 Code drops the replay:
    a thread's transcript is T3 Code's own, and an imported thread starts empty.
  * ``session/list`` offers this agent's top-level sessions on this host (in the
    client's cwd when given). Sessions created elsewhere are listed as
    ``omnigent:<session id>``, which ``session/load`` attaches to directly.
  * Sessions run on this machine only (``--host auto`` fails closed unless
    exactly one online Omnigent host matches this hostname), in the client's cwd.
  * Model and reasoning effort are config options; codex-native exposes a Plan
    mode backed by its collaboration mode. Other agents get T3 Code's Plan
    instructions as prompt text.
  * Omnigent approval requests become ``session/request_permission``, also
    between prompts.
  * File edits (Claude's Edit/MultiEdit/Write, Codex's apply_patch) are reported
    as ACP ``diff`` content. Edits carry the replaced snippet, not whole files, and
    Write shows as a new file.
  * ``--t3-tools`` (claude-native only) launches the TUI with the client's MCP
    servers. T3 Code reissues its MCP token when it reloads a thread, and a
    running TUI keeps its launch-time token, so the tools stop working until the
    TUI relaunches.
  * Updates that arrive while no prompt is running (turns started from another
    client, wakeups) are not forwarded; ACP has no idle turn to attach them to.
  * The stream has no replay, so every (re)connect reads the session snapshot:
    approvals still pending are re-raised, and a turn that ended while the
    stream was down is ended here. A deleted session (404/410) fails the running
    prompt and is not reconnected.
  * ``/fork-to HOST [CHECKOUT]`` (an ACP slash command) forks the Omnigent
    session and starts the fork on another online host (name, short name or
    prefix), in CHECKOUT under that host's home, by default the checkout of the
    same name as this one. The fork appears in the Omnigent app, and in that
    host's ``session/list`` as ``omnigent:<fork id>``; nothing reaches the agent.

Env: OMNIGENT_URL (default http://127.0.0.1:6767), OMNIGENT_ACP_LOG (log level).
"""

